 2. Trump admin moves to close China chip loophole 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Trump administration yesterday released a new rule to close a loophole that allows sensitive U.S. tech to reach China through data centers in the region. 

 Why it matters: The action comes after mounting pressure on the Commerce Department to block a top U.S. adversary from accessing semiconductors — technology that is central to the AI race. 

 Former Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's affair with a customer and alleged Chinese spy who works for Megaspeed — a data center company in Singapore — has intensified calls to tackle the diversion of chips to China. How it works: Before today, it was technically allowed for Chinese firms to access U.S. chips by renting them from data centers in Southeast Asia. 

 The rule targets data centers abroad that supply "significant volumes of computing power" to companies in high-risk countries, including China, Russia and Iran. The data centers have to vet their customers. The regulations, which take effect in 90 days, also require a license to export advanced chips to companies headquartered in countries with high smuggling risk, including Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Brazil. The intrigue: The interim final rule was released by the Commerce Department but "written by and at the direction of the Department of Treasury and the National Security Council," a news release states. 

 That language appears to nod to an ongoing and long-standing feud between Commerce Department Secretary Howard Lutnick and other top administration officials including Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent. "One wants to be friendly with China, and one wants to have a blowup with China," President Trump told reporters yesterday, referring to Lutnick and Bessent respectively.

