 3. Fed warns of AI boom&#x27;s growing financial risks 
 
 By Courtenay Brown and Neil Irwin 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Note: Survey of 20 market contacts in spring 2026 and 23 in fall 2026; Data: Federal Reserve ; Chart: Courtenay Brown The financial bets underpinning the AI boom are becoming far bigger and harder to untangle than soaring debt figures alone suggest.

 That is the new warning from the Federal Reserve, which, for the first time, devoted a section to AI financing in its latest assessment of financial stability . Why it matters: If AI investments fail to generate expected returns, the losses could spread beyond technology companies to the banks and investors financing them.

 The Fed's warning is not a response to the Nvidia turmoil that has unfolded in recent weeks. The report is published twice a year and largely reflects information available through Oct. 28 — before CEO Jensen Huang's departure. Driving the news: Fed chairman Kevin Warsh raised the possibility of these risks in opening remarks at a conference yesterday in San Francisco.

 "In time, the economy would recover, but without the productivity gains," Warsh said in prepared remarks . By the numbers: The Fed estimates that the largest U.S. banks have nearly $1 trillion in financial exposure to technology companies, data center developers and other businesses and lenders involved in the AI infrastructure boom.

 Companies borrowed more than $680 billion to finance data centers and other advanced computing infrastructure in the first 10 months of 2026 — roughly five times the amount raised in all of 2023, the Fed says. But other financial commitments have grown even faster: Seven big tech companies — including Nvidia, Microsoft and Oracle — have taken on more than $3 trillion in future lease payments, contracts and financial guarantees. Keep reading . 

 
 
